.\"+++
.\"
.\"	FACILITY:	MSCPHUB - MSCP disk server for OpenVMS nodes, over NISCA
.\"
.\"	MODULE:		doc/mscphub.8
.\"
.\"	ABSTRACT:	The manual page of the server.
.\"
.\"	AUTHOR:		StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan)
.\"
.\"	CREATION DATE:	18-OCT-2026
.\"
.\"	MODIFICATION HISTORY:
.\"
.\"		20-OCT-2026	RRL	X01-10 : Initial public version.
.\"		20-OCT-2026	RRL	X01-11 : /ACK=HOLD is the default.
.\"
.\"---
.TH MSCPHUB 8 "18 October 2026" "MSCPHUB @MH_IDENT@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mscphub \- MSCP disk server for OpenVMS nodes, over NISCA
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mscphub
.BI /CONFIG= file
.br
.B mscphub
.BI /INTERFACE= if
.BI /NODE= name
.BI /SYSTEMID= n
.BI /GROUP= n
.BI /AUTHORIZE= hex
.RI [ /qualifiers ]
.br
.B mscphub
.RB /HELP [=\(dq\fItopic\fR\ ...\(dq]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mscphub
lets OpenVMS nodes on the same LAN use Linux files and block devices as
their own disks.  To an OpenVMS node it looks like a disk controller of the
HSC kind: the node sees the disks with
.B SHOW DEVICE
and mounts them with
.BR MOUNT ,
one node or several at once
.RB ( MOUNT/CLUSTER ).
.PP
The server speaks NISCA, the protocol of PEDRIVER, on the Ethernet
(ethertype 60\-07).  It is an SCS node with the disk server MSCP$DISK and
without a connection manager: it is never a member of the VMScluster and
has no votes.  The OpenVMS nodes need no change \- the same group number
and cluster password, and a LAN adapter on the segment of the server.
.PP
A WRITE is answered only when its data is on the disk of the Linux host:
OpenVMS never asks a disk to flush a cache of its own.
.PP
A parameter is given on the command line or, one per line, in the file of
.BR /CONFIG ;
a line of the file that begins with
.B !
is a comment.
.BI \-\- NAME = value
may be written for
.BI / NAME = value\fR.
A parameter the server does not know is passed over with a warning \- read
the log after a change.
.SH PARAMETERS
.TP
.BI /CONFIG= file
Read the parameters from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI /INTERFACE= name
The Linux interface the OpenVMS nodes are on: an Ethernet adapter, a
bridge, a VLAN, a tap device in a bridge.  Mandatory.  The server needs
CAP_NET_RAW.
.TP
.BI /NODE= name
The SCSNODE of the server, 1 to 6 of A\-Z, 0\-9, $ and _.  Mandatory; no
other node of the group may have it.
.TP
.BI /SYSTEMID= n
The SCSSYSTEMID of the server, 1 to 65535.  Mandatory; it must differ in its
low 16 bits from that of every other node of the group.
.TP
.BI /GROUP= n
The cluster group number, 1 to 65279.  Mandatory.
.TP
.BI /AUTHORIZE= hex
The cluster password: the 8\-octet hash of SYS$SYSTEM:CLUSTER_AUTHORIZE.DAT
of an OpenVMS node, 16 hexadecimal digits, octet by octet.  Mandatory.
.TP
.BI /LISTEN= seconds
How long the server listens before it sends anything, 1 to 60; default 4.
A node that has the name or the system ID of the server stops it
(IDCLASH, NAMECLASH).
.TP
.BI /ALLOCLASS= n
The allocation class of the disks, 0 to 255; default 0.  With 42 the node
names the disk DKA100 of the server $42$DKA100; with 0, MHUB$DKA100.
.TP
\fB/UNIT0\fR ... \fB/UNIT7=\fIname\fB,\fIpath\fR[\fB,RO\fR][\fB,MEDIA=\fImedium\fR][\fB,SECTOR=\fIoctets\fR]
A disk.  The
.I name
is DK, a controller letter and a unit number: DKA100.  The
.I path
is a file \- its size is the size of the disk \- or a block device, opened
with O_DIRECT and O_EXCL: a device in use on the host is refused (DEVBUSY).
A device of 4096\-octet sectors is served; a write that does not fill whole
sectors is done as read\-modify\-write.
.B RO
serves it read only;
.B MEDIA=
names what the node is told the disk is \- RZ29 by default, RRD42 with
.BR RO ;
.B SECTOR=
takes a file as a disk of larger sectors, for tests only.  An ISO image of
the OpenVMS CD is served as it is, with
.BR RO .
.TP
.BR /SYNC= DSYNC | GROUP | NONE
When a WRITE is on the disk.
.B DSYNC
(the default): every write by itself (RWF_DSYNC).
.BR GROUP :
the writes that come while the disk is flushing share the next fdatasync.
.BR NONE :
as
.BR /UNSAFE .
.TP
.BI /CREDITS= n
How many commands a node may have in progress at once, 2 to 64; default 16.
.TP
.BR /ACK= HOLD | FLUSH
When the server acknowledges the packets of a node alone, when it has
nothing to send.
.B HOLD
(the default): only when the node asks, after 4 packets or on the 20 ms
tick; otherwise the acknowledgement goes with the next packet of the server.
.BR FLUSH :
as soon as the frames that came in are taken.
.TP
.BR /TIMEZONE= LOCAL | UTC | \fR[\fB+\fR|\fB\-\fR]\fIhh\fR[\fB:\fImm\fR]
The zone of the time the server tells the nodes (an OpenVMS node keeps
local time).
.B LOCAL
(the default) is the zone of the host.
.TP
.B /UNSAFE
Answer a WRITE as soon as Linux has its data in the page cache.  For tests
only: a crash of the host loses writes the node believes are done.
.TP
.B /PASSIVE
Only listen and report the nodes seen; send nothing.  Safe on any LAN.
.TP
.B /TRACE
Log every frame and every step of every channel.
.TP
.BI /LOGFILE= file
Write the messages to
.I file
rather than to the standard error.
.TP
.BR /HELP [=\(dq\fItopic\fR\ ...\(dq]
Display the help library: every parameter, the troubleshooting, every
message and what to do about it.
.B mscphub \(dq/HELP=Messages IOSTUCK\(dq
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop: HOSTSHUT on every virtual circuit, BYE to the group, the counters.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Log what the server is doing: UPTIME, a NODESTATS for each node (the
channel, the virtual circuit, the SCS connections), then the counters
STATS, TRSTATS, ACKSTATS, BLKSTATS, MSCPSTATS and, for each disk, UNITSTATS,
BKSTATS and LATSTATS (the time of its READ and WRITE commands).
.SH MESSAGES
Every message has the form of OpenVMS:
.PP
.RS
%MSCPHUB\-F\-IDCLASH, node ALPHA1 (08\-00\-2B\-E5\-40\-01) uses the system ID low word 65534 of this server
.RE
.PP
The letter is the severity: S, I, W, E, F (fatal \- the server stops).
.B mscphub \(dq/HELP=Messages\(dq
explains each of them and what to do.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @MH_CONFFILE@
The configuration of the service; the installation leaves it alone and
puts
.I @MH_CONFFILE@.example
next to it.
.TP
.I @MH_SYSTEMDDIR@/mscphub.service
The systemd unit: installed, not enabled.
.TP
.I @MH_FULL_HLBDIR@/mscphub.hlb
The help library of /HELP, and ru/mscphub_ru.hlb in Russian.
.TP
.I @MH_FULL_DOCDIR@/
The guide, in English and in Russian, and README.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the command line, listening only:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mscphub /PASSIVE /INTERFACE=eth1 /NODE=MHUB /SYSTEMID=65200 /GROUP=4242 /AUTHORIZE=5A3C9E1700000000
.fi
.RE
.PP
As a service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cp @MH_CONFFILE@.example @MH_CONFFILE@
vi @MH_CONFFILE@
systemctl enable \-\-now mscphub
systemctl kill \-s USR1 mscphub
journalctl \-u mscphub \-n 30
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The disk I/O is io_uring: a Linux kernel 5.10 or later, with io_uring not
turned off (kernel.io_uring_disabled).  Do not serve a file or a device the
host itself uses, and do not change one while it is served.
.SH AUTHOR
StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR tcpdump (8)
